      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Completely agree with this. I go to events where, say I only show the nearest 5 avatars, I may start at about 72+ FPS when I first launch. But as I load the 30-50 other avatars, the FPS may drop to 35-20 or lower FPS. \n\nGiven the recent mention of Blend Trees vs Layers for Animators, it must be true that the animators are the main CPU performance problem. I do not care if animators sliding in and out would case a minor hiccup in performance. I would prefer the option to choose between animators being on or off at my own discretion. \n\nAt the very least, if VRC is not going to enable this functionality, they should provide more infomation/a guide on how to swap to Blend Trees for Animators (brought my Animator render time from 0.5 ms to 0.1 ms by going from 25 to 4 layers).",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2022-08-20T02:06:09.066Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "As of build 1225 VRChat's Avatar Hider now only turns of the avatar's renderer when hiding avatars -- causing animators to still run.\nThis is to avoid state issues with animators using write defaults when the animator is turned on and off over and over again -- but can incur a decent CPU performance cost in some circumstances.\nWhile an in-game toggle to make avatar hider revert to the old behavior of turning everything, including the animator off, would likely come with too many caveats, for the time being, is it possible to add a launch  option flag to allow this behavior? (Something like `--avh-stop-animators`)\nAdding it as a flag would allow power users who'd like to squeeze every cycle of performance they can use the feature, while having it out of sight from most people who wouldn't quite understand the risks. Adding a logfile entry when the launch option is present should also help support point people in the right direction when it's on and the users are experiencing weirdness.",
